From 1f7562d0f7121c290b9d98d36a7745d3f743cea1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <claude@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 07:22:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Phase 4+5: configurable reconciliation schedule, AES-GCM file
 encryption for Logs and Outbox

Item 4 (Option A, as specified):
- Options/ServiceOptions.cs: OutboxOptions.ReconciliationIntervalMinutes
  (int, default 30, matching the value this was hardcoded to before -
  confirmed by re-reading the actual current Program.cs before touching
  it, which had been hand-edited to 3 for testing since the last time
  this file was reviewed).
- Program.cs: fail-fast validation (> 0), Cron.MinuteInterval(n) now
  reads the configured value instead of a literal.

Item 5 (fix properly, AES-GCM, as specified):

New Security/FileEncryptionService.cs: IFileEncryptionService (plain
string-in, string-out contract - deliberately no opinion about whether
a caller's unit of encryption is one log line or one whole file, since
Logger and Outbox need different chunking but the same primitive) plus
AesGcmFileEncryptionService, AES-256-GCM, format
"v1.{base64 nonce}.{base64 tag}.{base64 ciphertext}" - versioned,
dot-delimited, every field independently base64-encoded rather than
undocumented byte offsets. A fresh RandomNumberGenerator-sourced 12-byte
nonce on every single Encrypt call - this is the actual defect being
fixed, not just the algorithm: the previous TripleDES implementation
used a fixed all-zero IV, confirmed directly against real log files
where identical ciphertext appeared verbatim across files a year apart.
AesGcm's built into the BCL (System.Security.Cryptography) - no new
NuGet package needed. FileEncryptionFormatException (wrong shape
entirely) is kept distinct from CryptographicException (right shape,
failed the auth tag check) on purpose, so callers that need to
distinguish "this was never our format" from "this was tampered with"
can.

Options/ServiceOptions.cs: FileEncryptionOptions.Key (base64 AES-256
key). Program.cs: bound with the same fail-fast pattern as every other
options class here, plus an explicit decode-and-length check (must be
exactly 32 bytes) so a malformed key fails at startup, not on first
encrypt attempt. Key is a dedicated secret for this purpose alone -
never PrintAgentAuth's or ReconciliationCredential's key reused - same
one-key-per-purpose reasoning already applied everywhere else in this
service. User-secrets for dev, environment variable for production,
same as those two.

Logs: lan/Logger.cs's AppendLog now calls IFileEncryptionService.Encrypt
instead of CLS.ENCRYPTION.Enc_TripleDES, injected via a new constructor
parameter threaded through all four constructor overloads. Confirmed by
grep before this change: Logger is constructed in exactly one place
(Program.cs's singleton registration), so this was a fully contained
change. The "[Username:...]" prefix the old implementation always
included is dropped - CLS.GLOBALS._loginUname is never actually set
anywhere in this codebase (confirmed by grep in the design-review phase
before this was ever touched), so it was always empty; not worth
carrying an always-empty field forward into the new format.

Outbox: Outbox/OutboxStore.cs's FileOutboxStore now encrypts the whole
JSON payload on SaveAsync/UpdateAsync (whole-file, not per-line like
Logger - an entry is written once, read once, deleted once, so there's
no append-in-place concern). GetAllAsync uses
IFileEncryptionService.LooksEncrypted as a cheap, non-throwing shape
check to decide whether to decrypt-then-parse or parse-directly.
Backward compatibility, explicitly determined rather than assumed:
this store has never encrypted anything before now, so any file already
pending is plaintext JSON - confirmed by reading the store's own
pre-existing implementation, not inferred. No migration/backfill
performed - not justified when recognizing both formats on read is
smaller and safer, and any entry that gets rewritten via UpdateAsync
after this phase (e.g. a failed reconciliation attempt) is written back
out encrypted from that point on, achieving the same end state
organically. The existing corrupt-file handling in GetAllAsync's catch
block (already there for JsonException, to keep one bad file from
taking down the whole sweep) is extended to also catch
FileEncryptionFormatException and CryptographicException the same way -
same treatment, skip and leave in place for manual inspection.

OutboxReconciliationJob.cs: NOT modified at all. It only ever calls
_store.GetAllAsync() and receives a list - all decryption and
backward-compatibility handling lives entirely inside FileOutboxStore,
which is the actual file-access boundary. This is the smallest possible
surface for "only modify the file access/decryption boundary, preserve
existing reconciliation business logic, retry behavior, idempotency,
and error handling" - achieved by not touching the file at all rather
than touching it minimally.

Explicitly out of scope, flagged rather than silently left inconsistent:
LocalBatch.lbt (the card batch file) still uses the old
ENCRYPTION.Enc_TripleDES/GLOBALS._KEY_CONFIG path. The specification
covers "Log Files" and "Outbox," not the batch file - a different
purpose entirely, and changing it wasn't asked for.

NOT implemented this phase, per the investigation finding presented
before any code was written: the Super Admin decryption endpoint.
Confirmed by repo-wide grep that no SuperAdmin/SystemAdmin concept
exists anywhere in this service - it has exactly one auth scheme and
one policy (PrintAgentOnly), validated against its own dedicated key.
The only place IsSystemAdmin exists in this whole distributed system is
the Inventory API, validated against its own shared Jwt:SigningKey - a
key this service has never held and, per every other decision in this
project, deliberately shouldn't, since holding it would let a
compromised branch machine forge full tenant/admin sessions rather than
just the narrowly-scoped things it can forge today. "Reuse the existing
Super Admin mechanism" has nothing to reuse within this repository;
implementing it requires a real decision between a security-regressing
shortcut and a properly-scoped new cross-service credential, and that
decision is not implementation's to make silently.

No dotnet SDK in the authoring environment - this phase is lower-risk
than the Hangfire phase in one specific respect (AesGcm needs no new
NuGet package, it's pure BCL) but touches more files than any single
prior phase. Verified by: brace balance across every touched and new
file; JSON validity on appsettings.json; the full-repo symbol-table
cross-check (same four already-known false positives, nothing new); a
repo-wide grep confirming Logger is constructed in exactly one place
before changing its constructor signature; and a line-by-line review of
the AesGcm API calls (nonce/ciphertext/tag/plaintext parameter order for
both Encrypt and Decrypt) against the documented BCL signature, since
this is the one piece of this phase I could not exercise against a real
compiler. Please build and test locally before merging - this is
cryptographic code, and while the format and API usage have been
checked as carefully as I can without a compiler, only a real build and
a real encrypt/decrypt round-trip test can fully confirm it.
